class Solution {
  public int prefixCabinetFloorTicket(String[] words, int k) {
    String[] a=words; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {List<Map<Character,Integer>>next=new ArrayList<>();next.add(new HashMap<>());List<Integer>counts=new ArrayList<>();counts.add(0);for(String word:a){int node=0;for(char c:word.toCharArray()){if(!next.get(node).containsKey(c)){next.get(node).put(c,next.size());next.add(new HashMap<>());counts.add(0);}node=next.get(node).get(c);counts.set(node,counts.get(node)+1);}}for(String word:a){int node=0,s=0;for(char c:word.toCharArray()){node=next.get(node).get(c);s+=counts.get(node);}out.add(s);}}
    int answer=out.isEmpty()?0:out.get(0);for(int x:out)answer=Math.min(answer,x);return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
The working idea

For each lowercase word, consider each of its nonempty prefixes and count how many input words begin with that prefix, including repeated words and the word itself. Produce the sum of these counts for each word in input order.

Return the smallest value in the derived sequence, or 0 for an empty sequence.

Why this works

Each derived value follows the unit's stated order and includes exactly the items allowed by its definition. The receipt then folds or filters those values without changing their meaning. Check the empty, singleton, repeated and maximum-size inputs before trusting the final total.

Target time O(total characters); space O(n).

